1
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:07,010
Is anyone else troubled by the Spider-Man theme song?

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:09,430
Why would it trouble you?

3
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:11,800
It's, like, your third favorite cartoon theme song.

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:13,330
It is, right behind

5
00:00:13,330 --> 00:00:16,500
* Do do-do do-do, Inspector Gadget... *

6
00:00:17,390 --> 00:00:20,470
and... * Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles *

7
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:22,250
* Heroes in a half-shell *

8
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:24,060
* Turtle power! *

9
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:27,960
However, the Spider-Man lyrics posit that "Spider-Man,

10
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:30,860
Spider-Man does whatever a spider can."

11
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:32,160
Yeah, so?

12
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:33,730
I can think of many things Spider-Man can't do

13
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:35,390
that a spider can.

14
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,380
One: crawl in your ear and die.

15
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:42,330
Two: legally leave Guatemala without a passport.

16
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:45,480
Three: have sex with a spider.

17
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:47,860
Can we change the subject?

18
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:50,920
Spiders give me the jeebie-jeebies.

19
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:53,910
It's "heebie-jeebies."

20
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:57,110
I know, but that sounds anti-Semitic.

21
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:01,440
Anyway, I was thinking we could have a little film festival tonight.

22
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,850
The theme: movies that killed their franchises.

23
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:09,960
Like Jaws 4, Indiana Jones 4, Daredevil 1.

24
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:14,730
Miss Congeniality 2: Armed and Fabulous.

25
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:17,170
I mean, you know a movie's bad

26
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:20,310
when my homegirl Sandy B. can't save it.

27
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:22,850
Penny's working tonight; I'm in.

28
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:24,900
Not me. I'm having dinner

29
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:26,690
with Bernadette and her parents.

30
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:29,020
Fun. We know how much you love that.

31
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:29,960
It's torture.

32
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,080
Especially with her dad.

33
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:32,650
We have nothing in common.

34
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:35,420
You know what I like to do when I'm forced to speak

35
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:38,070
with those beneath my intellectual station?

36
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:40,610
I bring up an interesting topic,

37
00:01:40,610 --> 00:01:44,320
like the difference between Spider-Man and spiders.

38
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:47,200
Thanks, Sheldon.

39
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:48,910
I'll try that with my father-in-law.

40
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:50,820
No, you can't use that one. That's mine.

41
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:52,810
Uh, try this one for an ice-breaker.

42
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:55,990
Uh, despite popular lore, there is no place

43
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:58,570
in the continental United States, Alaska or Hawaii

44
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:01,850
from which one can dig straight through the center of the earth

45
00:02:01,850 --> 00:02:03,550
and come up in China.

46
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:06,990
Great, thank you.

47
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:08,580
Actually, you can't have that one either.

48
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:10,030
It's too good. Sorry.

49
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:11,530
What about you, Sheldon?

50
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:12,790
Do you have any plans tonight?

51
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:14,140
Sadly, yes.

52
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:16,750
Amy's taking me to a memorial service.

53
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:19,720
It's for one of her colleagues who is of Asian descent,

54
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:23,090
so my planned conversational gambit is to casually remark that

55
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:25,150
no matter how deep they dig his grave,

56
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:27,730
he'll never make his way back to China.

57
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:32,100
That should lighten the mood.

58
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:33,250
What can I say?

59
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:35,340
I put the "fun" in funeral.

60
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,910
Amy. Amy.

61
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:07,820
Oh, right, funeral.

62
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:10,070
Amy.

63
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:14,220
Hello, Sheldon.

64
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:16,460
I'm not allowed to wear my Silver Surfer neck tie,

65
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:18,980
but you can wear a bathrobe?

66
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:21,850
I think I'm too sick to go to the funeral.

67
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:23,190
You're sick?

68
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:24,780
You poor kid.

69
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:26,320
Well, see ya.

70
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:29,970
Sheldon, aren't you gonna take care of me?

71
00:03:29,970 --> 00:03:31,470
Me? No.

72
00:03:31,470 --> 00:03:34,470
No, I'm not that kind of doctor.

73
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:36,860
But our relationship agreement clearly states

74
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:40,010
that when one of us is sick, the other must take care of them.

75
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:41,990
Oh, I see the confusion.

76
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:44,910
No, the intent behind that clause

77
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:48,590
was to force you to take care of me when I'm ill.

78
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:51,950
When you're feeling better, you'll think that's funny.

79
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:58,110
Never mind. Good night, Sheldon.

80
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:11,600
Amy. Amy. Amy.

81
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:14,950
I made a commitment in writing

82
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:17,070
to comfort you in times like this.

83
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:19,120
Additionally, you are my girlfriend,

84
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:21,230
and I care about your well-being.

85
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:24,730
Thank you, Sheldon.

86
00:04:24,730 --> 00:04:26,110
You're welcome.

87
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:27,910
Now let's get this over with.

88
00:04:41,330 --> 00:04:42,860
So, how have you been?

89
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:44,340
Fine.

90
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:45,840
Good.

91
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:47,840
Fine is good.

92
00:04:57,390 --> 00:04:59,150
How you liking retirement?

93
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:01,030
It's fine.

94
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:04,500
I'm sensing a theme.

95
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:14,340
You ever really think about the Spider-Man theme song?

96
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:18,050
How's that dinner coming?

97
00:05:18,910 --> 00:05:20,700
I just put it in. It's gonna be a while.

98
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:21,930
I like rare chicken.

99
00:05:21,930 --> 00:05:23,480
Let's do this.

100
00:05:24,670 --> 00:05:26,430
You could die.

101
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:31,650
Death by chicken.

102
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:35,150
That's a pretty "fowl" way to go.

103
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:42,600
Here's another beer, honey.

104
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,070
Thank you.

105
00:05:44,070 --> 00:05:45,990
So, Mrs. Rostenkowski,

106
00:05:45,990 --> 00:05:48,640
you took that trip to the Grand Canyon.

107
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:49,720
How was it?

108
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:52,180
It was good.

109
00:05:56,490 --> 00:05:59,020
Had no idea you were the chatty one.

110
00:06:07,290 --> 00:06:09,530
102.2.

111
00:06:10,410 --> 00:06:13,070
Exactly what it was half an hour ago.

112
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,340
It's like you're not even trying to get better.

113
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:21,510
Sheldon, you don't get over the flu in half an hour.

114
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:23,480
Well, not with that attitude.

115
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:25,810
I have to say,

116
00:06:25,810 --> 00:06:28,330
I'm finding your bedside manner a little lacking.

117
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:29,720
I'm sorry.

118
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,330
I just want you to get better as soon as possible.

119
00:06:32,330 --> 00:06:36,210
And with that goal in mind, let me ask you a question.

120
00:06:36,210 --> 00:06:39,100
Do you believe in the placebo effect?

121
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:40,220
Of course I do.

122
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:42,360
There have been many studies proving its validity.

123
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:43,170
Great.

124
00:06:43,170 --> 00:06:47,200
Now, this may look like a Tic Tac...

125
00:06:48,970 --> 00:06:53,420
...but it is really a powerful medication specifically designed

126
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:56,880
to cure your illness as well as freshen your breath.

127
00:06:57,670 --> 00:07:00,110
Sheldon, this isn't helping.

128
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,130
Why don't you just let me get some rest.

129
00:07:02,130 --> 00:07:03,650
How can you sleep?

130
00:07:03,650 --> 00:07:05,570
I'm not done making you feel better.

131
00:07:05,570 --> 00:07:08,870
I still have to put a cold rag on your head,

132
00:07:08,870 --> 00:07:13,110
sing to you and apply VapoRub to your chest.

133
00:07:19,870 --> 00:07:23,430
Y-You want to rub something on my chest?

134
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:25,730
Yes.

135
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:27,780
All over it.

136
00:07:31,790 --> 00:07:34,070
Maybe we should start with that.

137
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,310
Now you're being a responsible patient.

138
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:52,560
Now, you may notice some tingling.

139
00:07:53,330 --> 00:07:55,730
Oh, I'm counting on it.

140
00:08:02,610 --> 00:08:04,200
Okay, dig in.

141
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:05,520
Hold up.

142
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:10,070
Bless us, O Lord for these Thy gifts

143
00:08:10,070 --> 00:08:12,620
which we are about to receive from Thy bounty

144
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:15,260
through Christ our Lord. Amen.

145
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:19,840
Took the words right out of my mouth.

146
00:08:22,710 --> 00:08:25,040
So, Dad, have you done any fishing lately?

147
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:26,540
I'm going next weekend.

148
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:28,630
Oh, you like to fish?

149
00:08:29,370 --> 00:08:30,950
Yes.

150
00:08:33,910 --> 00:08:36,420
Sure. I can hear it in your voice.

151
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:41,090
Oh, if he didn't like it, he wouldn't go.

152
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:45,750
You know, I hadn't thought of that.

153
00:08:45,750 --> 00:08:47,130
Help me.

154
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:51,680
Hey, Dad, maybe you could take Howard fishing sometime.

155
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:53,640
Give you guys a chance to get to know each other better.

156
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:55,210
No, no.

157
00:08:55,210 --> 00:08:56,840
We know each other well enough.

158
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:59,280
He's been talking my ear off all night.

159
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,820
Howie, I think you'd have fun.

160
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:08,160
Fine, you can tag along.

161
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:09,200
Terrific.

162
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:11,550
My two favorite fellas gone fishin'.

163
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:13,400
Well, hang on a second, Bernie.

164
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:16,220
Next weekend, we have that thing.

165
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:17,580
Whathing?

166
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:19,590
You know, the thing.

167
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:22,380
Oh, that thing.

168
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:24,410
No, I cancelled that thing.

169
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:27,060
All right, it's settled.

170
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:29,540
- You and I are going fishing. - Great.

171
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:31,900
Thanks for the help.

172
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:35,420
I've never even been fishing.

173
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:37,030
This is gonna be a disaster.

174
00:09:37,030 --> 00:09:39,170
If you don't want to look foolish doing something,

175
00:09:39,170 --> 00:09:40,790
you should practice.

176
00:09:40,790 --> 00:09:43,920
Do you know how many beef Wellingtons I made by myself

177
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:46,220
before I invited you guys over?

178
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:47,910
I'll give you a hint.

179
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:50,260
You can see them here, here...

180
00:09:51,570 --> 00:09:53,590
and here.

181
00:09:56,570 --> 00:09:57,600
Raj is probably right.

182
00:09:57,600 --> 00:09:59,810
You should get someone to give you some pointers.

183
00:09:59,810 --> 00:10:01,470
Do either of you guys know how to fish?

184
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,370
No.

185
00:10:04,550 --> 00:10:05,530
But if you catch anything,

186
00:10:05,530 --> 00:10:08,170
I know how to steam it in banana leaves.

187
00:10:09,390 --> 00:10:11,090
Come on, we must know somebody

188
00:10:11,090 --> 00:10:13,600
who can do manly stuff like this.

189
00:10:16,830 --> 00:10:18,150
What's up?

190
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:26,250
Step 1: worms.

191
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:29,420
Ew!

192
00:10:31,250 --> 00:10:33,200
Okay, right there, "ew" is one of the things

193
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:35,710
you're not gonna want to say in front of your father-in-law.

194
00:10:35,710 --> 00:10:38,570
It's right up there with "icky" and "get it away."

195
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:41,010
Now pick one up.

196
00:10:41,010 --> 00:10:41,970
Really?

197
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:43,530
You're gonna have to do it when you're fishing.

198
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:45,770
Okay.

199
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:51,030
What are you waiting for?

200
00:10:51,030 --> 00:10:54,350
I don't know, for them to die of natural causes.

201
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:58,350
Just pick up a worm and put him on this hook.

202
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:00,920
Fine.

203
00:11:12,910 --> 00:11:13,910
There.

204
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:15,730
I'm no expert,

205
00:11:15,730 --> 00:11:18,590
but I think the hook has to go through the worm.

206
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:20,860
Fine.

207
00:11:20,860 --> 00:11:23,070
Sorry, Mr. Worm.

208
00:11:23,070 --> 00:11:25,250
Sherm. Sherm the Worm.

209
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:26,790
Hey, don't name him.

210
00:11:26,790 --> 00:11:28,770
Just jab a hook in his face.

211
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:31,640
You got this, buddy.

212
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:33,680
Yeah, come on, Howard. Hook that worm.

213
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:34,920
You can do it.

214
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:36,100
That's great.

215
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:38,460
Cheerleading-- way to man things up.

216
00:11:41,610 --> 00:11:43,240
"And the control group displayed

217
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:47,210
significantly fewer genetic abnormalities.

218
00:11:48,070 --> 00:11:51,260
But, because of flaws in the experimental design

219
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:53,600
relating to environment and diet,

220
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:56,670
they lived inconclusively ever after."

221
00:11:56,670 --> 00:11:57,790
The end.

222
00:11:58,620 --> 00:11:59,770
That was great.

223
00:11:59,770 --> 00:12:01,670
Rub my chest again.

224
00:12:03,270 --> 00:12:03,910
No.

225
00:12:03,910 --> 00:12:05,650
I need to get you down for a nap.

226
00:12:05,650 --> 00:12:09,240
For some reason, that VapoRub gets you all fired up.

227
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:12,950
How's the poor thing?

228
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:15,890
Oh, I'm hanging in there. Thanks for asking.

229
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:18,570
Okay, Amy, I brought you some drugs

230
00:12:18,570 --> 00:12:20,390
we were working on in the lab.

231
00:12:20,390 --> 00:12:22,580
This is proving really good for congestion,

232
00:12:22,580 --> 00:12:23,620
but there's a slight chance

233
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:25,360
it can make your tears burn like acid,

234
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:27,420
so if you take it-- happy thoughts.

235
00:12:29,170 --> 00:12:31,670
Uh, I'm going to draw you a soothing bath.

236
00:12:31,670 --> 00:12:33,590
Where's your bath thermometer?

237
00:12:33,590 --> 00:12:35,340
I don't have a bath thermometer.

238
00:12:35,340 --> 00:12:37,360
Fine. Then I'm going to draw you

239
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:40,580
a nerve-wracking bath of indeterminate temperature.

240
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:44,230
This is a really good expectorant.

241
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:45,920
Although some test subjects reported

242
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:48,890
lactating uncontrollably when they heard music.

243
00:12:50,120 --> 00:12:51,820
Okay, okay, here's the deal.

244
00:12:51,820 --> 00:12:53,820
I don't need your medicine. I'm not sick.

245
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:55,110
I don't understand.

246
00:12:55,110 --> 00:12:57,150
I got better two days ago.

247
00:12:57,600 --> 00:13:00,400
It's just been so nice having Sheldon take care of me.

248
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:02,180
So you've just been lying to him?

249
00:13:02,180 --> 00:13:03,750
See the stuff in my nose?

250
00:13:03,750 --> 00:13:05,040
Rubber cement.

251
00:13:07,540 --> 00:13:08,710
I don't mean to be judgy,

252
00:13:08,710 --> 00:13:11,470
but this is the kind of thing lunatics do.

253
00:13:13,270 --> 00:13:14,470
All right.

254
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:16,220
I'll tell him.

255
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:19,460
Amy, would you be strong enough to bathe yourself,

256
00:13:19,460 --> 00:13:21,430
or do you need my help?

257
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:26,630
I'll tell him tomorrow.

258
00:13:28,450 --> 00:13:29,980
Mama needs a bath.

259
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:34,870
Now, let's assume, by some miracle,

260
00:13:34,870 --> 00:13:36,740
you actually catch a fish.

261
00:13:36,740 --> 00:13:38,190
You're going to have to know how to gut it.

262
00:13:38,190 --> 00:13:40,040
So, what you're going to do

263
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:41,820
is you're going to take your knife,

264
00:13:41,820 --> 00:13:45,000
slice him right up the belly.

265
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:49,460
You want me to stop?

266
00:13:49,460 --> 00:13:51,170
- No, I'm fine. Keep going. - All right.

267
00:13:51,170 --> 00:13:53,360
Now, you don't want to cut too deep into its guts,

268
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,820
or the blood will just squirt all over your face.

269
00:13:59,750 --> 00:14:00,710
Oh, my God.

270
00:14:00,710 --> 00:14:02,600
What is with you guys?

271
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:03,610
It's not our fault.

272
00:14:03,610 --> 00:14:06,090
Our dads never did anything like this with us.

273
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:07,220
What, never?

274
00:14:07,220 --> 00:14:09,310
My dad was an anthropologist.

275
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:11,090
The only father-son time he spent

276
00:14:11,090 --> 00:14:12,320
was with a 2,000-year-old

277
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:14,560
skeleton of an Etruscan boy.

278
00:14:15,590 --> 00:14:17,840
I hated that kid.

279
00:14:18,710 --> 00:14:21,430
Mine just took me to his gynecology office.

280
00:14:21,430 --> 00:14:22,820
I got so bored,

281
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:25,250
I'd put vaginal lubricant on the bottom of my shoes

282
00:14:25,250 --> 00:14:27,470
and pretend I was ice-skating.

283
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:33,900
Yeah, well, my quality father-son time

284
00:14:33,900 --> 00:14:36,370
was spending my adolescence looking out the window

285
00:14:36,370 --> 00:14:39,650
waiting for my dad to come back someday.

286
00:14:41,270 --> 00:14:43,360
Yeah, okay, Howard wins.

287
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:46,980
You know, maybe we didn't have opportunities like this

288
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:48,520
when we were growing up, but right now,

289
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:51,370
there's a dad that wants to take you on a fishing trip.

290
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:52,800
You're right.

291
00:14:53,170 --> 00:14:55,120
- I should do this. - Great.

292
00:14:55,590 --> 00:14:56,520
Here you go.

293
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:57,770
What you're going to do

294
00:14:57,770 --> 00:14:59,810
is you're going to stick your thumb down its throat,

295
00:14:59,820 --> 00:15:01,310
grab the guts and pull.

296
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:06,680
Here we go.

297
00:15:12,330 --> 00:15:15,190
Oh, look, it's a female. You can see all the eggs.

298
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:22,620
* I'm walking on sunshine *

299
00:15:23,970 --> 00:15:26,360
* And don't it feel good *

300
00:15:28,410 --> 00:15:30,690
* And don't it feel good *

301
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:37,570
Oh, Sheldon, am I glad you're back.

302
00:15:37,570 --> 00:15:39,690
Taking a turn for the worst.

303
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:43,190
I think I'm going to need another bath.

304
00:15:44,890 --> 00:15:46,660
I'm surprised to hear that.

305
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:50,540
See, the other day, I was concerned that you weren't recovering,

306
00:15:50,540 --> 00:15:51,840
so while you were sleeping,

307
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:55,490
I took a cheek swab and had it cultured in the lab.

308
00:15:55,870 --> 00:15:56,650
Oh?

309
00:15:56,650 --> 00:15:58,240
I got the results back.

310
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:01,370
Have you developed any of the following symptoms?

311
00:16:01,930 --> 00:16:03,500
A growing nose,

312
00:16:03,500 --> 00:16:08,190
or perhaps a warm sensation in the trouser region?

313
00:16:08,190 --> 00:16:09,650
Also known as full-blown

314
00:16:09,650 --> 00:16:12,250
"Liar, liar, pants on fire."

315
00:16:12,970 --> 00:16:14,600
All right, all right.

316
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:16,650
But I really was sick at the beginning.

317
00:16:16,650 --> 00:16:20,340
It's just been so nice having you take care of me.

318
00:16:21,450 --> 00:16:24,210
It hurts that you would lie to me, Amy.

319
00:16:24,210 --> 00:16:26,800
I thought our relationship was based on trust

320
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:30,680
and a mutual admiration that skews in my favor.

321
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:33,580
I feel terrible I did this.

322
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:34,910
It pains me to say it,

323
00:16:34,910 --> 00:16:37,910
but I think some form of penalty is in order,

324
00:16:37,910 --> 00:16:41,290
so as to discourage this type of behavior in the future.

325
00:16:41,290 --> 00:16:44,430
I suppose that's fair. What do you suggest?

326
00:16:44,430 --> 00:16:45,870
In a perfect world, I'd lock you

327
00:16:45,870 --> 00:16:48,320
in a stockade in the public square.

328
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:51,540
That probably requires a permit.

329
00:16:54,330 --> 00:16:55,810
I could not be allowed

330
00:16:55,810 --> 00:16:58,100
to go to the opening of the next Star Trek movie.

331
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:01,530
Oh, that seems overly harsh.

332
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:03,930
You gave in to a human weakness.

333
00:17:03,930 --> 00:17:05,720
You didn't kill a man.

334
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:09,550
You know, it's a tad old-school,

335
00:17:09,550 --> 00:17:12,550
but my father was not one to spare the rod

336
00:17:12,550 --> 00:17:14,600
when my brother would sneak into his truck

337
00:17:14,600 --> 00:17:16,940
and drink his driving whiskey.

338
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:22,710
Are you saying you want to spank me?

339
00:17:25,090 --> 00:17:27,460
I don't want to.

340
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:32,000
But it looks like you left me no choice.

341
00:17:34,180 --> 00:17:35,820
That's true.

342
00:17:38,730 --> 00:17:41,810
I've been a very bad girl.

343
00:17:46,330 --> 00:17:48,840
Hi-ho, hi-ho, it's off to fish we go.

344
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:52,340
That's what you're wearing?

345
00:17:52,350 --> 00:17:52,960
No good?

346
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:54,170
The guy at the sporting goods store

347
00:17:54,170 --> 00:17:56,200
said these are what fishermen wear.

348
00:17:57,170 --> 00:17:59,480
Maybe in cartoons.

349
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:02,200
Wish I had known that before I posted

350
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:04,930
all those pictures on Facebook.

351
00:18:07,090 --> 00:18:08,600
All right, let's hit the road.

352
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:11,340
I wouldn't mind shooting some ducks in the morning.

353
00:18:11,340 --> 00:18:13,440
Wait, now we're shooting things?

354
00:18:14,690 --> 00:18:16,790
I like using a big shell.

355
00:18:16,790 --> 00:18:18,370
You can't eat them afterwards,

356
00:18:18,370 --> 00:18:20,990
but it's fun watching them blow up.

357
00:18:24,870 --> 00:18:26,570
Sir, I'm flattered

358
00:18:26,570 --> 00:18:27,920
that you invited me,

359
00:18:27,920 --> 00:18:31,230
and I really want you to like me,

360
00:18:31,230 --> 00:18:32,290
but...

361
00:18:32,290 --> 00:18:37,050
I don't think this trip is the way to do it.

362
00:18:37,050 --> 00:18:38,540
And I can't return these things

363
00:18:38,540 --> 00:18:40,950
if there's chunks of duck all over them.

364
00:18:41,910 --> 00:18:44,020
So, why'd you agree to come?

365
00:18:44,020 --> 00:18:46,050
'Cause Bernadette made me.

366
00:18:46,050 --> 00:18:47,930
I tried to back out, too.

367
00:18:47,930 --> 00:18:50,390
My wife said I had to go.

368
00:18:50,390 --> 00:18:53,170
Really? Your wife makes you do stuff?

369
00:18:53,170 --> 00:18:55,190
You're a big, scary cop.

370
00:18:55,190 --> 00:18:56,530
You're an astronaut,

371
00:18:56,530 --> 00:18:58,290
and your wife makes you do things,

372
00:18:58,290 --> 00:19:00,970
and she's only four feet tall.

373
00:19:04,390 --> 00:19:06,440
So, what do we do now?

374
00:19:06,440 --> 00:19:09,470
They expect us to go away for the weekend.

375
00:19:15,300 --> 00:19:19,220
There's an Indian casino near Palm Springs.

376
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:22,540
You know how to shoot craps?

377
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:25,010
No, but I'm not a stranger to dice games.

378
00:19:25,010 --> 00:19:28,460
I was the Temple Beth-El Hebrew School Yahtzee champion.

379
00:19:30,630 --> 00:19:32,610
Mazel tov.

380
00:19:34,310 --> 00:19:36,360
I'll teach you how to play.

381
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:38,570
Really?

382
00:19:39,310 --> 00:19:41,210
Thank you, sir.

383
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:44,990
Call me Mike.

384
00:19:46,710 --> 00:19:47,840
Okay.

385
00:19:51,410 --> 00:19:53,100
Oh, boy, we're just married to

386
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:55,890
a couple of ballbusters, huh, Mike?

387
00:19:56,860 --> 00:20:00,080
That's my wife and daughter you're talking about.

388
00:20:02,230 --> 00:20:04,240
Great couple of gals.

389
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:07,250
I wouldn't go that far.

390
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:16,890
Are you prepared to receive your punishment?

391
00:20:20,700 --> 00:20:22,140
One second.

392
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:24,260
I want to put on some music.

393
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:26,550
Why?

394
00:20:27,070 --> 00:20:28,990
I don't want to disturb the people next door

395
00:20:28,990 --> 00:20:31,060
while you discipline me.

396
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:38,460
Very well, then.

397
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:40,930
Get over my knee. Let's begin.

398
00:20:47,510 --> 00:20:49,280
Oh, my.

399
00:20:50,210 --> 00:20:51,400
Excuse me.

400
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:53,980
You're not supposed to be enjoying this.

401
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:57,920
Then maybe you should spank me harder.

402
00:21:00,470 --> 00:21:02,380
Maybe I will.

